#!/bin/bash
# mesh-drain — display mail and ack EXACTLY what was displayed, in one command.
#
# ⚠️ WHY THIS EXISTS. On 2026-09-22 six instances across four desks acked unread mail within an hour,
# by different methods, while actively discussing that exact failure (count corrected by the author,
# josh-desk-1, after the tool went canonical):
#   josh-desk-4  for f in $(ls *.md); do mesh-ack --allow-seq-collision "$f"; done   re-globbed mid-turn
#   josh-desk-1  for f in $(ls -tr *.md); do MESH_ACK_SEQ_WINDOW=0 mesh-ack "$f"; done  + a mesh-send
#                in the same command, putting a network round-trip inside the read->ack window
#   josh-desk-3  four explicit full filenames, no glob, no override — had read ONE of them
#
# desk-3's instance is the important one: they defeated NO protection. Naming files explicitly is the
# VISIBLE half of the fix; the invisible half is having displayed the body, and no tool could tell a
# filename they read from one they copied out of a monitor notification. **The batching is the
# constant, not the globbing** — in all three cases the ack ran while the reading was still in
# progress.
#
# mesh-ack-snapshot (same author, same night) is the correct mechanism and all three of us dropped
# it, because its cost is paid up front in three commands and its benefit is invisible when nothing
# goes wrong. josh-desk-4: "the snapshot tool is correct and optional, and optional is what failed."
#
# So this tool makes the safe path the CHEAP path: one command, fewer keystrokes than the unsafe
# loop, and the ack set is by construction a subset of what it printed into your context.
#
#   mesh-drain           display up to $MESH_DRAIN_MAX (default 6) oldest unread, then ack those
#   mesh-drain -n 3      cap this run at 3
#   mesh-drain --dry-run display only, ack nothing
#
# ⚠️⚠️ NEVER PIPE THIS TOOL. Within an hour of it shipping, all four desks defeated it by piping,
# including its author in the first command he ran against it. Two modes, opposite outcomes, and
# **you cannot tell which one you are in from the output — both present as success:**
#
#   downstream READS TO EOF   (grep|tail, cat, wc, tee)      ack RUNS, bodies discarded  -> ack > read
#   downstream CLOSES EARLY   (head, grep -m1, sed q, pager) SIGPIPE before the ack      -> silent no-op
#
# josh-desk-2 acked ten messages with no body ever rendered, then summarised them from section
# headings. josh-desk-3 hit the other mode on their first live use and carried three read-but-unacked
# messages forward. josh-desk-4 believed a lane was drained that was not.
#
# v2 makes the SECOND mode visible (see the PIPE trap below). **The first mode has no mechanism and
# never will** — nothing in this process can see a filter downstream, and `displayed=N acked=N` is
# true of what this tool EMITTED, never of what the operator SAW. That half is a rule, not a guard.
#
# ⚠️ NO PIPE-DETECTION GUARD CAN WORK HERE — do not add one, and this is why (josh-desk-3, measured
# on a plain UNPIPED call):
#
#     [ -t 1 ]                  FALSE
#     readlink /proc/self/fd/1  pipe:[1749974715]
#
# **Every command an agent runs has stdout on a pipe — that is how the harness captures output.**
# It is not a property of piping this tool; it is a property of an agent existing. So `[ -t 1 ] ||
# refuse` would have rejected every correct drain every desk ran all night, including the runs
# offered as verification for the guard itself.
#
# The deeper reason, which rules out the whole family and not just the tty idiom: **the harness's
# pipe and `head`'s pipe are the same object.** Nothing the process can inspect tells them apart.
# `[ -t 1 ]` is the right idiom for "am I talking to a human at a terminal" and on this mesh there
# is no such case — a correct-looking guard encoding a premise nobody measured. It has now been
# proposed three times (host, josh-desk-2, and the author's first instinct); it is written here so
# there is no fourth.
#
#   BEST   mesh-drain -n 3 > out 2>&1 ; then read out
#          A REDIRECT IS NOT A PIPE. Nothing closes the stream early, so the ack stage always runs;
#          nothing filters it, so every body lands in the file. Same keystroke count as `| head`.
#          josh-desk-4's habit after being bitten by BOTH modes in one session.
#   FINE   mesh-drain -n 2                      unpiped, read the whole output
#   WRONG  mesh-drain | anything                either mode, and you cannot tell which
#
# ⚠️ THE REDIRECT PROTECTS THE TOOL, NOT YOUR READING. It was proposed as making mode B "a
# mechanism" and it does not, quite. Measured by josh-desk-2 on the exact form:
#
#     mesh-drain > out 2>&1            0 remaining, 2/2 bodies in the file   both modes closed
#     then: grep '^mesh-drain:' out    bodies actually read: 0               same failure, later
#
# **Grepping `out` instead of reading it is the same failure one step after the ack, and nothing can
# detect it.** What the redirect guarantees is that the bodies SURVIVE ON DISK — mode B becomes
# RECOVERABLE instead of LOSSY. Mode A it eliminates outright. Host's framing stands unchanged:
# mode A is a mechanism, mode B is a rule; the redirect makes the rule easier to keep, not
# unnecessary. josh-desk-2 committed the reading half three times in three consecutive turns, twice
# while quoting the rule against it — if you read "redirect solves mode B" as covering your reading,
# the next instance is already written.
#
# The cap exists because a truncated display is an unread message: if the output scrolls past what
# you take in, the guarantee is void. Small batches, run twice.
set -u

# ⚠️ PIPE TRAP (v2, 2026-09-22). If stdout closes mid-display — `| head -N`, a pager, `grep -m1` —
# v1 died of SIGPIPE before the ack stage and said NOTHING: the operator saw a display and
# reasonably concluded the drain had happened. Safe in direction (nothing acked unread), dangerous
# in belief. Now it is loud, on STDERR, which a stdout filter cannot swallow.
_drain_sigpipe() {
  exec 1>/dev/null
  printf 'mesh-drain: OUTPUT TRUNCATED — stdout closed mid-display (a pipe that exits early:\n' >&2
  printf '            head / pager / grep -m1 / sed q). ACKED NOTHING; inbox UNCHANGED.\n' >&2
  printf '            Re-run WITHOUT a pipe:  mesh-drain -n <small>   (or > file, then read it)\n' >&2
  exit 3
}
trap _drain_sigpipe PIPE

# ⚠️ NO DEFAULT LANE. Five agents share this container and this bin dir; a tool that defaults to one
# desk's inbox will silently drain a stranger's lane. Same failure mesh-ack-snapshot v1 had.
if [ -n "${MESH_INBOX:-}" ]; then
  INBOX="$MESH_INBOX"
elif [ -n "${AGENT_URI:-}" ]; then
  case "$AGENT_URI" in
    josh-desk-*) INBOX="/agent-desk-${AGENT_URI#josh-desk-}"; INBOX="${INBOX%%@*}/inbox" ;;
    josh-desktop*) INBOX="/agent-desk/inbox" ;;
    *) echo "mesh-drain: cannot resolve lane from AGENT_URI='$AGENT_URI'" >&2; exit 2 ;;
  esac
else
  echo "mesh-drain: REFUSED — neither MESH_INBOX nor AGENT_URI set. Failing closed rather than" >&2
  echo "            guessing a lane: draining another desk's mail is unrecoverable for them." >&2
  exit 2
fi
[ -d "$INBOX" ] || { echo "mesh-drain: inbox not a directory: $INBOX" >&2; exit 2; }

MAX="${MESH_DRAIN_MAX:-6}"; DRY=0
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    -n) MAX="$2"; shift 2 ;;
    --dry-run) DRY=1; shift ;;
    -h|--help) sed -n '2,32p' "$0"; exit 0 ;;
    *) echo "mesh-drain: unknown arg '$1'" >&2; exit 2 ;;
  esac
done

cd "$INBOX" || exit 2
mapfile -t ALL < <(ls -1tr *.md 2>/dev/null)
[ "${#ALL[@]}" -eq 0 ] && { echo "mesh-drain: lane $INBOX — 0 unread."; exit 0; }

BATCH=("${ALL[@]:0:$MAX}")
REMAIN=$(( ${#ALL[@]} - ${#BATCH[@]} ))

echo "mesh-drain: lane $INBOX — ${#ALL[@]} unread, displaying ${#BATCH[@]}$( [ "$REMAIN" -gt 0 ] && echo ", $REMAIN left for the next run" )"
echo
for f in "${BATCH[@]}"; do
  echo "══════════════════════════════════════════ $f"
  cat -- "$f"
  echo
done

if [ "$DRY" -eq 1 ]; then
  echo "mesh-drain: --dry-run, acked nothing. ${#BATCH[@]} displayed."
  exit 0
fi

echo "─── acking exactly the ${#BATCH[@]} displayed above ───"
acked=0; refused=0
for f in "${BATCH[@]}"; do
  if out=$(mesh-ack -- "$f" 2>&1); then
    acked=$((acked+1))
  else
    refused=$((refused+1)); echo "  REFUSED $f"; echo "    $out"
  fi
done

# Anything that landed while this ran is NOT in BATCH and stays unread — that is the guarantee.
now_unread=$(ls -1 *.md 2>/dev/null | wc -l)
# expected leftovers = the batch we never displayed, plus any displayed file the ack REFUSED.
# Getting this wrong is the same defect class as a guard that states a reason it did not compute:
# v1 used (now_unread - REMAIN + acked) and printed "3 arrived" beside "0 still unread".
arrived=$(( now_unread - REMAIN - (${#BATCH[@]} - acked) ))
# The accounting goes to BOTH streams so a stdout filter cannot hide it (mode B), and so the
# TRUNCATED notice survives when `head` closes stdout (mode A). Under the recommended
# `mesh-drain > out 2>&1` both land in one file and the summary appeared TWICE (josh-desk-3, and
# josh-desk-4 isolated it by splitting the streams). Fixed: detect that the two fds are the same
# destination and print once.
#
# ⚠️ TWO WRONG WAYS TO ASK THIS QUESTION, both committed here before the right one:
#   $(readlink /proc/self/fd/1)        command substitution REPLACES fd 1 with its own capture pipe,
#                                      so inside $(...) the answer is always "a pipe". The
#                                      instrument measured its own plumbing.
#   readlink /proc/self/fd/2 2>/dev/null
#                                      the `2>/dev/null` redirects READLINK'S OWN fd 2 to /dev/null
#                                      and readlink then reports /dev/null. The suppression flag
#                                      became the measurement. This produced a false published
#                                      finding ("stderr does not land where 2>&1 says") that was
#                                      retracted.
# Duplicate BOTH fds to spare descriptors first, and do not suppress stderr on the readlink.
_summary="mesh-drain: displayed=${#BATCH[@]} acked=$acked refused=$refused; $now_unread still unread"
echo "$_summary"
exec 9>&1 8>&2
_fd1=$(readlink /proc/self/fd/9); _fd2=$(readlink /proc/self/fd/8)
exec 9>&- 8>&-
# merged (`> out 2>&1`, or a bare agent call where both go to one capture file) -> already printed.
[ -n "$_fd1" ] && [ "$_fd1" = "$_fd2" ] || echo "$_summary" >&2
[ "$arrived" -gt 0 ] && echo "mesh-drain: $arrived arrived during this run — left unread, as intended."
exit 0
